delete the rung-one webview; the browser build mounts the native chat

t3-lens-threads 4.4. Both of `apps/desktop`'s entries now mount the same native
T3 view, so the Electron `<webview>` fallback has no host left to serve.

Gone: the `<webview>` branch in the chat dock and `chat/webview.d.ts`;
`webviewTag: true` in the main process; `frame-src http://127.0.0.1:*` from the
renderer CSP; `pairingUrl` from `t3SessionSchema`, from `dispatch/chat.ts`'s
output, from `@rennet/t3-chat`'s `SidecarSession`, and the
`POST /api/auth/pairing-token` exchange in `t3/supervisor.ts` that minted it —
nothing else consumed that credential.

`vite.browser.config.ts` gets what `vite.renderer.config.ts` has: the `~` alias
into `vendor/t3code/apps/web/src`, `dedupe: ["react", "react-dom"]`, the two
`import.meta.env` defines the vendored source reads at module scope, and
`assetsInclude: ["**/*.wasm"]`. `src/browser/entry.tsx` provides
`T3ChatSlotProvider` with `T3NativeChat` and `T3ThreadView`, exactly as
`src/renderer/index.tsx` does.

Evidence, from `pnpm nx run rennet-desktop:build`: `dist/browser/assets/` now
carries `native-chat-*.js` (1.1M) and `native-chat-*.css` (380K) at the same
content hashes as `dist/renderer/assets/` — the same lazy chunk, built twice
from the same source.

Absence test: the dock's DOM case that used to render the `<webview>` — the
daemon answers, no host provides the components — now asserts an honest line and
`querySelector("webview") === null`. Load-bearing on the guest never returning:
restoring the branch reddens both. NOT load-bearing on the pairing token, and
the comment says so — `pairingUrl` is off the wire schema entirely, so the
fixture cannot offer one to begin with.

A host that provides no components shows "This host does not mount the chat
view." rather than an empty box.
